---
description: Keep TSX thin; put state and complex logic in colocated hooks
globs: src/**/*.{ts,tsx}
alwaysApply: false
---

# Thin React components

`.tsx` files compose UI. They may take props, hold DOM refs, call hooks, derive simple view flags (`showEmpty`, `showError`), and return JSX.

Move into a colocated `use*.ts` hook next to the component:

- `useState` and derived state
- `useEffect` / `useLayoutEffect`
- Async work and loading/error status
- Gesture and keyboard handling
- Handlers with branches, awaits, or more than a couple of statements

Follow `usePdfDocument.ts`, `useDocumentPan.ts`, and `useDocumentZoom.ts`.

A component may keep a one-line adapter that only forwards to a prop or ref (e.g. toolbar file-input click). If a handler grows, extract it.

Pure calculations with no React state belong in plain modules (`panGesture.ts`, `zoomAnchor.ts`); hooks call them.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — pan state and pointer handlers inline in the component
function DocumentView() {
  const [isPanning, setIsPanning] = useState(false)
  const onPointerDown = (event) => { /* ... */ }
  // ...
}

// ✅ GOOD — hook owns behavior; component wires JSX
function DocumentView() {
  const scrollRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null)
  const pan = useDocumentPan(scrollRef)
  return (
    <div
      ref={scrollRef}
      onPointerDown={pan.onPointerDown}
      onPointerMove={pan.onPointerMove}
    />
  )
}
```
